✅ 1. What is Polymorphism in C++?
Polymorphism = “one interface, many forms.”
It allows the same function call to behave differently depending on the type of object.
Example:
shape->area() behaves differently for Circle, Square, Triangle.

✅ 2. Types of Polymorphism
A. Static (Compile-Time) Polymorphism
Achieved using:
-->Function Overloading
-->Operator Overloading
-->Templates (also compile-time binding)
Binding happens during compile time.

B. Dynamic (Run-Time) Polymorphism
Achieved using:
-->Virtual Functions
-->Function Overriding
-->Interfaces / Abstract classes
Binding happens using virtual table (vptr + vtable) at runtime.

✅ 3. Why Polymorphism is Important?
| Benefit                 | Explanation                                             |
| ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Extensibility**       | Add new derived classes without changing existing code. |
| **Maintainability**     | Code becomes cleaner and follows SOLID principles.      |
| **Loose coupling**      | Works with dependency injection.                        |
| **Reusability**         | Write generic code for multiple object types.           |
| **Runtime flexibility** | Decide behavior dynamically.                            |


✅ 4. Where Do We Use Polymorphism?
You use polymorphism whenever you want common interface, different behavior:
✔ GUI: Button, Checkbox, Slider all override draw()
✔ Game dev: Enemy, Player, Boss override update()
✔ Drivers: USB keyboard, mouse, pen drive override connect()
✔ DB APIs: MySQL, Postgres override query()
✔ Logging: FileLogger, ConsoleLogger override log()
✔ Payment systems: UPI, Card, Wallet override pay()



✅ 5. Dependency Injection & Polymorphism
Dependency Injection works only because of polymorphism.

Example:
class Payment {
  public:
    virtual void pay(int amount) = 0;
};
class UPI : public Payment {
  public:
    void pay(int amount) override {
        cout << "Paid using UPI: " << amount << "\n";
    }
};
class CreditCard : public Payment {
  public:
    void pay(int amount) override {
        cout << "Paid using Credit Card: " << amount << "\n";
    }
};

// DI: Constructor injection
class PaymentProcessor {
  private:
    Payment* method; // depends on *interface*, not on concrete class
  public:
    PaymentProcessor(Payment* p) : method(p) {}
    void process(int amt) {
        method->pay(amt);
    }
};

Here, PaymentProcessor does not care if UPI or CreditCard is used.
✔ Loose coupling
✔ Easy unit testing
✔ Replace behavior anytime



🟦 6. Static Polymorphism Example – Function Overloading
Program
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
class Printer {
  public:
    void print(int x) { cout << "Printing int: " << x << endl; }
    void print(double x) { cout << "Printing double: " << x << endl; }
    void print(string s) { cout << "Printing string: " << s << endl; }
};
int main() {
    Printer p;
    p.print(10);
    p.print(3.14);
    p.print("Hello");
}
/*
Output
Printing int: 10
Printing double: 3.14
Printing string: Hello
*/

🟩 7. Static Polymorphism Example — Operator Overloading
Program
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
class Complex {
  public:
    int real, imag;

    Complex(int r=0, int i=0) : real(r), imag(i) {}

    Complex operator + (const Complex& other) {
        return Complex(real + other.real, imag + other.imag);
    }
};
int main() {
    Complex a(2, 3), b(4, 5);
    Complex c = a + b;
    cout << c.real << " + " << c.imag << "i";
}
/*
Output
6 + 8i
*/


🟥 8. Dynamic Polymorphism Example (Virtual Functions)
Program (with overriding)
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
class Shape {
  public:
    virtual void draw() {             // virtual = dynamic binding
        cout << "Drawing generic shape\n";
    }
    virtual ~Shape() {}               // always use virtual destructor
};
class Circle : public Shape {
  public:
    void draw() override {
        cout << "Drawing Circle\n";
    }
};
class Square : public Shape {
 public:
    void draw() override {
        cout << "Drawing Square\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    Shape* s1 = new Circle();
    Shape* s2 = new Square();

    s1->draw();
    s2->draw();

    delete s1;
    delete s2;
}
/*
Output
Drawing Circle
Drawing Square
*/


🟧 9. Real-Life Polymorphism Example + Dependency Injection
Payment System (Best Interview Example)
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
// Interface
class Payment {
  public:
    virtual void pay(int amount) = 0;
    virtual ~Payment() {}
};

// Derived classes
class UPI : public Payment {
  public:
    void pay(int amount) override {
        cout << "Paying using UPI: " << amount << "\n";
    }
};

class Card : public Payment {
  public:
    void pay(int amount) override {
        cout << "Paying using Card: " << amount << "\n";
    }
};

// Dependency Injection + Polymorphism
class PaymentProcessor {
    Payment* method; // base class pointer
  public:
    PaymentProcessor(Payment* p) : method(p) {}

    void doPayment(int amt) {
        method->pay(amt);
    }
};

int main() {
    UPI upi;
    Card card;

    PaymentProcessor p1(&upi);
    PaymentProcessor p2(&card);

    p1.doPayment(500);
    p2.doPayment(2000);
}
/*
Output
Paying using UPI: 500
Paying using Card: 2000
*/

10. Key Interview Points on Polymorphism
❗ MUST KNOW LIST (Short & Powerful)
✔ Static vs Dynamic Polymorphism
→ Overloading vs Overriding
→ Compile-time vs Run-time
→ No virtual table vs virtual table

✔ Virtual Table (vtable)
→ Each object has a hidden pointer vptr
→ Points to table of virtual functions

✔ When to use virtual destructor
→ When deleting objects via base pointer

✔ Abstract classes vs interfaces
→ Pure virtual functions
→ Cannot instantiate

✔ Overloading rules
→ Same function name
→ Different parameter list
→ Return type cannot differ alone

✔ Overriding rules
→ Must have same signature
→ Must use virtual in base
→ Use override keyword (best practice)

✔ Covariant return types
→ Derived class pointer return allowed

✔ Dependency Injection + Polymorphism
→ Pass base class pointer/reference
→ Runtime behavior selection
→ Enables unit testing

✔ SOLID principles
OCP & DIP rely heavily on polymorphism

🟩 Final Short Summary
Polymorphism allows:
One interface → many behaviors
Compile-time or run-time flexibility
Extensible code
Dependency injection
Decoupled architectures
Polymorphism is core to OOP and critical for scalable, testable, maintainable C++ applications.



/* ========================================================================================= */

✅ 1. vptr / vtable Diagram (Easy to Understand)

When you use virtual functions, C++ automatically adds:

vptr → A hidden pointer inside every polymorphic object

vtable → A table of function pointers for virtual functions

🔷 Example Class
class Base {
public:
    virtual void show() {}
};

class Derived : public Base {
public:
    void show() override {}
};

🎯 Memory Diagram — vptr + vtable
➤ Object Memory Layout
   ┌─────────────────────┐
   │  vptr (hidden) -----|----------┐
   └─────────────────────┘          |
   │  Base object data (if any)     |
   └─────────────────────┘          |
                                    |
                                ┌───────────────┐
                                │   vtable      │
                                ├───────────────┤
Base vtable:                    │ &Base::show   │
                                └───────────────┘

➤ Derived object layout
   ┌─────────────────────┐
   │  vptr --------------|-------------------------------┐
   └─────────────────────┘                               |
   │  Derived object data                                |
   └─────────────────────┘                               |
                                                         |
                                                ┌────────────────┐
                                                │   vtable       │
                                                ├────────────────┤
Derived vtable:                                  │ &Derived::show│
                                                └────────────────┘

How dynamic dispatch works?

When you call:

Base* b = new Derived();
b->show();


Steps:

b contains pointer to Derived object.

Derived object contains vptr → Derived vtable.

C++ checks the vtable entry for show.

Calls Derived::show().

💥 Without vptr/vtable = no runtime polymorphism

That’s why functions must be virtual for dynamic polymorphism.

✅ 2. Overloading vs Overriding – Complete Comparison Table
| Feature         | Overloading                     | Overriding                      |
| --------------- | ------------------------------- | ------------------------------- |
| Type            | Compile-time polymorphism       | Run-time polymorphism           |
| Where?          | Same class                      | Base & Derived classes          |
| Requirement     | Same name, different parameters | Same name, same signature       |
| Virtual needed? | ❌ No                            | ✔ Required (virtual keyword)    |
| vtable used?    | ❌ No                            | ✔ Yes                           |
| Return type     | Can differ                      | Must be same (except covariant) |
| Purpose         | Convenience                     | Runtime behavior change         |
| Binding         | Early binding                   | Late binding                    |


Simple example:
Overloading:
void fun(int);
void fun(double);


Overriding:
class Base { virtual void fun(); };
class Derived : public Base { void fun() override; };



✅ 3. Abstract Class vs Interface – Interview Table
| Feature                         | Abstract Class                      | Interface (Pure Abstract Class)    |
| ------------------------------- | ----------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| Contains non-virtual functions? | ✔ Yes                               | ❌ No                               |
| Contains data members?          | ✔ Yes                               | ❌ Ideally no                       |
| Pure virtual functions          | Can have both normal & pure virtual | Must have all pure virtual         |
| Constructors                    | ✔ Allowed                           | ✔ Allowed (but unusual)            |
| Multiple inheritance            | Can only inherit one                | Many interfaces can be implemented |
| Purpose                         | Common base + shared implementation | Common behavior only               |


Code Example:
//Abstract Class
class Animal {
  public:
    void eat() {}             // has implementation
    virtual void sound() = 0; // pure virtual
};

//Interface
class ISound {
  public:
    virtual void sound() = 0; // only pure virtual
};



✅ 4. Design Patterns That Use Polymorphism
Below are the three most important patterns for interviews.

🏭 A. Factory Pattern (Object Creation using Polymorphism)
Used when:
Object creation logic must be abstracted.
Return objects via base pointer.

//Code:
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
class Transport {
  public:
    virtual void deliver() = 0;
    virtual ~Transport() {}
};
class Truck : public Transport {
  public:
    void deliver() override { cout << "Deliver by Truck\n"; }
};
class Ship : public Transport {
  public:
    void deliver() override { cout << "Deliver by Ship\n"; }
};
// Factory
class TransportFactory {
  public:
    static Transport* create(string type) {
        if (type == "truck") return new Truck();
        else return new Ship();
    }
};
int main() {
    Transport* t = TransportFactory::create("truck");
    t->deliver();
    delete t;
}
/*
Output:
Deliver by Truck
*/



🎯 B. Strategy Pattern (Replace Behavior at Runtime)
Used when:
You need to switch algorithm dynamically.
Example: Sorting strategies, payment strategies, compression strategies.

//Code:
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
class Strategy {
  public:
    virtual void execute() = 0;
    virtual ~Strategy() {}
};
class Aggressive : public Strategy {
  public:
    void execute() override { cout << "Aggressive Attack\n"; }
};
class Defensive : public Strategy {
  public:
    void execute() override { cout << "Defensive Position\n"; }
};
// Context using DI
class Player {
    Strategy* strategy;
  public:
    Player(Strategy* s) : strategy(s) {}
    void setStrategy(Strategy* s) { strategy = s; }
    void action() { strategy->execute(); }
};
int main() {
    Aggressive ag;
    Defensive df;

    Player p(&ag);
    p.action();

    p.setStrategy(&df);
    p.action();
}
/*
Output:
Aggressive Attack
Defensive Position
*/


🧾 C. Visitor Pattern (Add new behavior without modifying classes)
Used when:
You have many classes
You want to add new operations on them
Without modifying original classes (Open-closed principle)

//Code:
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
class Circle;
class Square;

class Visitor {
  public:
    virtual void visit(Circle*) = 0;
    virtual void visit(Square*) = 0;
};
class Shape {
  public:
    virtual void accept(Visitor* v) = 0;
};
class Circle : public Shape {
  public:
    void accept(Visitor* v) override { v->visit(this); }
};
class Square : public Shape {
  public:
    void accept(Visitor* v) override { v->visit(this); }
};
class DrawVisitor : public Visitor {
  public:
    void visit(Circle*) override { cout << "Drawing Circle\n"; }
    void visit(Square*) override { cout << "Drawing Square\n"; }
};
int main() {
    Circle c;
    Square s;
    DrawVisitor dv;

    c.accept(&dv);
    s.accept(&dv);
}
/*
Output
Drawing Circle
Drawing Square
*/

🎉 Final Summary
✔ Polymorphism requires vptr + vtable
✔ Overloading = compile-time, Overriding = runtime
✔ Abstract class = partial implementation
✔ Interface = only pure virtual functions
✔ Factory pattern uses polymorphism to create objects
✔ Strategy pattern uses polymorphism to switch runtime behavior
✔ Visitor pattern adds new behavior without modifying classes




